WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.367 --> 00:00:06.253
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:17.180
This is the skull of a pachycephalosaur,

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:20.540
a 16 foot long plant eater.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.540
Despite years of research, we're still

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:29.627
trying to understand why it had this bizarre shaped head.

00:00:29.627 --> 00:00:32.501
[GROWLING]

00:00:38.455 --> 00:00:40.830
The most distinctive feature about the pachycephalosaurs,

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.832
of course, is this really weird dome

00:00:42.832 --> 00:00:44.040
that they have on their head.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:51.620
All around it is a series of very old tubercles and nobles

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:52.940
and bobbles.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:57.080
They look quite dragon-like.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:59.720
When they first saw these very thick domed skulls--

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.230
and these domes could be up to 30 centimeters in thickness--

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.093
they were thought to be very strong.

00:01:05.093 --> 00:01:07.010
It was thought that maybe they were using them

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:12.840
for headbutting each other in contests over social hierarchy.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:19.620
Today, animals like bighorn sheep butt heads in this way.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.590
A thickened skull helps protect their brain from impact.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:30.080
But does this fully explain pachycephalosaur's domed head?

00:01:33.862 --> 00:01:35.320
A number of paleontologists thought

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:38.380
they should look inside the skulls.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:41.387
They sliced up a number of pachycephalosaur domes.

00:01:41.387 --> 00:01:42.970
And when they did that, they found out

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:44.830
that maybe it wasn't strong enough

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:49.130
to withstand these direct head on impacts.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:52.090
However, they did discover something else,

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:56.300
suggesting the dome had a different use.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:01.800
The surface was marked with a mesh of tiny fibers.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.830
Perhaps they supported brightly colored skin

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:08.400
used to attract a mate.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:15.300
This new theory seemed to be accepted until 2013,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:20.060
when a new discovery was made.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.790
A pachycephalosaur was found with marks

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:25.820
showing it had survived multiple injuries

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:29.210
to the top of its head.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:32.570
And it wasn't a one-off.

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:34.973
They went back and looked through historic museum

00:02:34.973 --> 00:02:36.890
collections and discovered that actually there

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:38.900
was quite a lot of evidence for these lesions

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.330
in a similar place across a whole bunch

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:44.390
of different pachycephalosaurs.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:47.120
This is good evidence that they probably were head butting.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:57.070
So were the skulls strong enough to protect pachycephalosaur's

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:58.210
brain after all?

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.860
To find out, scientists turned to the latest

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:07.870
scanning technology.

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:11.560
We can use CT scans to build virtual models of the bones.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:14.770
And then we can subject those to various engineering tests

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:17.020
in the same way that we do with an airplane

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:20.210
part or an artificial hip.

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:23.840
This provided the final piece of evidence.

00:03:27.708 --> 00:03:29.750
What they've shown is that pachycephalosaur domes

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:34.690
could actually have withstood an enormous amount of force.

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:38.030
It's a sort of force that goes through American footballers

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:39.500
when they hit each other.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:48.910
We now believe pachycephalosaurs used their head

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:54.505
both as a formidable weapon, and as a colorful display--

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:00.700
two ways to demonstrate who was boss

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.720
in their prehistoric world.

